    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d616">

                <pb facs="0681" n="586" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg586"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6124: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-07T18:00:00+00:00">April 7, 1936—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 7—2:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>186. Atherton discussed Department’s 115, April 5, 8 p.m., this afternoon with
                    Sir Victor Wellesley<anchor n="88" xml:id="d616-f1"/> and Orde.<anchor n="89"
                        xml:id="d616-f2"/> The contents of section 1 were admitted to be
                    substantially correct, and it was added <pb facs="0682" n="587" xml:id="pg587"/>
                    with reference to paragraph 2 of section 1 that Leith-Ross<anchor n="90"
                        xml:id="d616-f3"/> had, after an unofficial survey on the spot, recommended
                    to the bondholders the acceptance of the proposed adjustment for the
                    Tientsin–Pukow loan. Foreign Office informally claimed that the security and
                    provisions behind the Hukuang loan agreement were no better than those behind
                    the Tientsin–Pukow agreement …</p>
                <p>Part 2 did not arise.</p>
                <p>As for part 3, both Wellesley and Orde were reluctant to hazard an opinion as to
                    whether the Foreign Office would agree to undertake to make suggestions to the
                    Bondholders Committee in their direct and purely unofficial negotiations with
                    the Chinese Government over obligations which had been a long time in default.
                    However, they would endeavor to give the Embassy a reply as soon as possible
                    after the forthcoming holidays.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d616-f1" n="88" xml:id="d616-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Victor A. A. H. Wellesley, Deputy Under Secretary of State, British Foreign
                    Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d616-f2" n="89" xml:id="d616-f2-content">
                <p>Charles William Orde, Counselor and Head of the Far Eastern Department, British
                    Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d616-f3" n="90" xml:id="d616-f3-content">
                <p>Sir Frederick Leith-Ross, Chief Economic Adviser to the British Government and
                    temporary financial adviser to the British Ambassador in China since 1935; see
                        <ref target="frus1935v03#pg_591"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1935, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, p.
                        591</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
